## Android scroll performance & frosted chrome (April 2026)

**What we learned**

- **Symptom:** On Android (dev APK / dev client), **Home** and **Insights** scrolling felt **janky / low frame rate** compared to **Profile** and **Habit Management**; **iOS** stayed smooth.
- **Why:** **`expo-blur`** `BlurView` on Android is **much more expensive** than on iOS. Worst case: **fixed** frosted chrome (e.g. Home **`ScrollableDateHeaderBar`** overlay) with **content scrolling underneath** — the blur must update every frame. Screens where the chrome **scrolls away with the page** (Profile, Habits) avoid that “live blur over moving pixels” pattern and felt better. Insights also stresses the GPU with a long **SectionList** + chart-heavy rows; removing blur from chrome still helps consistency.
- **Fix:** **`TabBarBlurBackground`** uses **`BlurView` only on iOS**. On **Android**, use a **`View`** with **`StyleSheet.absoluteFill`** and solid **`ANDROID_CHROME_SOLID_BACKGROUND`** from **`constants/glassChrome.js`** ( **`colors.cardBackground`** ). Keep existing **frost overlays** in **`GlassChromeBar`** and **`MainTabBar`** so Android stays softly tinted, not only flat white. Remove **`getGlassBlurAndroidProps`** and stop passing **`experimentalBlurMethod` / `blurReductionFactor`** for Android blur tuning — no longer needed.
- **Single place to change:** **`TabBarBlurBackground`** is shared by **`GlassChromeBar`** (top strips: date header, Profile, Insights, etc.) and **`MainTabBar`** (bottom tab bar).

**Testing**

- **Android:** Scroll **Home** and **Insights**; confirm improvement. Check headers + **bottom tab bar** look acceptable (solid + frost, not iOS blur).
- **iOS:** Headers and tab bar should still show **real blur** (unchanged).
- Optional: validate on an Android **release** build; dev can exaggerate jank.

